Design Document: Decoding Base64 Encoded Query Parameters in ASP.NET CoreObjectiveProvide a solution for decoding base64 encoded query parameters in incoming HTTP requests. Two approaches are presented: a middleware-based solution for a global application, and an action filter-based solution for targeted application within specific controllers or actions.Approach 1: Middleware-Based SolutionOverviewImplement a middleware component that intercepts all HTTP requests, checks for base64 encoded query parameters, decodes them, and reconstructs the request URL with the decoded values.ImplementationMiddleware Class (Base64QueryParameterMiddleware):Inspect each query parameter in incoming HTTP requests.Decode parameters that are base64 encoded.Reconstruct the request URL with decoded parameters.Registering Middleware:Add the middleware to the application's request processing pipeline in Startup.cs.Pros and ConsPros:Global application ensures all base64 encoded parameters are decoded.Transparent to controllers/actions, requiring no additional attributes or configuration.Cons:Applies to all requests, which may not always be desired.Potentially decodes parameters that should not be decoded, if not base64 encoded by design.Approach 2: Action Filter-Based SolutionOverviewCreate an action filter that decodes base64 encoded query parameters and applies this logic selectively to specific controllers or actions.ImplementationAction Filter Class (DecodeBase64QueryParamsAttribute):Inspect and decode base64 encoded query parameters.Provide decoded values to the action method via modified RouteData or directly to action parameters.Applying the Action Filter:Globally across all controllers/actions.Selectively to specific controllers or actions using attribute tagging.Pros and ConsPros:Selective application allows for targeted decoding.Keeps parameter handling logic closer to where it is used, improving cohesion.Cons:Requires explicit decoration of controllers/actions, potentially leading to missed decodings if not applied.Slightly more complex setup compared to the middleware approach.Code ExamplesMiddleware Examplepublic class Base64QueryParameterMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public Base64QueryParameterMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    public async Task Invoke(HttpContext context) { /* Implementation */ }
}

// In Startup.cs
app.UseMiddleware<Base64QueryParameterMiddleware>();
Action Filter Examplepublic class DecodeBase64QueryParamsAttribute : ActionFilterAttribute
{
    public override void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext context) { /* Implementation */ }
}

// Usage
[DecodeBase64QueryParams]
public IActionResult MyAction() { /* Implementation */ }
ConclusionBoth solutions offer a viable approach to decoding base64 encoded query parameters in ASP.NET Core applications. The choice between middleware and action filters should be guided by the scope at which decoding needs to be applied, the desire for global vs. targeted decoding, and the need for maintaining cohesion within controller logic.
